 to detect different  parts  of clothes and to learn feature  embeddings to find similar products  there's always a domain gap between the  inner wild and catalog  images so make sure that the embedding  we learned can  accommodate for that recently we have  expanded feature to  recognize also home furniture there are  many additional challenges  such as handling occluded objects and  different perspectives  we use 3d synthetic data to help in the  training process to bridge the gap  speaking of us 3d another direction that  our team are working on  is to let customer view products in 3d  in their own environments  using arguments reality technologies a  very big pinpoint for online shopping  is that they're not able to see the  product from every angle  let alone sit in their room and the  worst shopping experience is that  when you brought the item and you found  it does not fit you have to return it  so we developer ai technology to address  this problem  the first thing we need to do is  obviously the model creation fortunately  fredrik's team has generated many 3d  furniture models  that can directly rendering air however  objects like this  the one that i'm showing here have  complex structure texture and  material and requires a lot of time for  humans to model so we work with the  images science team to introduce  a scanning process and a new model  format to encode  object line fields at runtime we use our  in-house light field rendering  techniques  to render those models in 3d once you  enter the ar experience from the detail  page  the next step is to determine the  position on orientation of the camera  with respect to customer's room  so we use the visual initial odometry  system to  produce both the camera posts as well as  jack surface for customer to place  virtual objects on  on top of the zinc geometry information  we have been trying to add more semantic  understanding of  environments to bad cut better guide  customers through the experience  a 